Is over 10 10 runs.

In this section, we prove that the MRP estimator has a lower variance than the LR estimator under some smoothness conditions. This variance reduction property of the MRP estimator parallels that of the RP estimator ( Gal, 2016 ; Xu et al., 2019 ) . Specifically, we show that the marginal variance of MRP can be expressed or controlled by the variance of the corresponding RP estimator for the individual components. Through this reduction, we can focus on analyzing the variance of the individual components, which is much easier to work with. While the following results can be easily extend to the full reinforcement learning setting with regularization, we consider the bandit setting without regularization for the ease of presentation: J ⁡ ( π 𝜽 ) = 𝔼 a ∼ π 𝜽 ​ [ r ⁡ ( a ) ] . \displaystyle J({\pi_{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}})=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim{\pi_{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}}}[r(a)]. (19) The entropy term can also be included by redefining r ⁡ ( a ) r(a) to be the sum of the reward and sample entropy. We focus on analyzing the gradient of different estimators with respect to the distribution parameters and the weighting probabilities of Gaussian mixture policies. In this case, the mixture policy can be expressed as π 𝜽 m ​ ( a ) = ∑ k = 1 N π 𝜽 w w ​ ( k ) ​ π 𝜽 b k b ​ ( a ) = ∑ k = 1 N w k ​ 𝒩 ​ ( a , μ k , σ k 2 ) {\pi_{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}^{m}}(a)=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\pi^{w}_{{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}^{w}}(k)\pi_{{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}^{b_{k}}}^{b}(a)=\sum_{k=1}^{N}w_{k}\mathcal{N}(a;\mu_{k},\sigma_{k}^{2}) with 𝜽 = [ 𝜽 b 1 ⊤ , ⋯ , 𝜽 b N ⊤ , 𝜽 w ⊤ ] ⊤ = [ [ μ 1 , σ 1 ] ⊤ , ⋯ , [ μ N , σ N ] ⊤ , [ w 1 , ⋯ , w N ] ⊤ ] ⊤ ∈ ℝ 3 ​ N { m{\mathbf{	heta}}}=\left[\smash{{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}^{b_{1}}}^{	op},\cdots,\smash{{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}^{b_{N}}}^{	op},\smash{{ m{\mathbf{	heta}}}^{w}}^{	op}ight]^{	op}=\left[[\mu_{1},\sigma_{1}]^{	op},\cdots,[\mu_{N},\sigma_{N}]^{	op},[w_{1},\cdots,w_{N}]^{	op}ight]^{	op}\in\mathbb{R}^{3N} , where μ k \mu_{k} , σ k \sigma_{k} , and w k w_{k} may depend on shared pa